= Replication =

== Overview ==
'''Replication''' is the repetition or re-investigation of a scientific question using newly obtained observations, data, participants, experiments, or study execution in order to test whether a finding can be obtained again.

Terminology differs among disciplines.

Following the National Academies 2019 convention, this article distinguishes replication from computational [[Reproducibility|reproducibility]]:

'''Replication''' — obtaining consistent results across studies aimed at answering the same scientific question, each using its own data.

== Why replication matters ==
One study can be affected by:
* sampling variation;
* hidden bias;
* measurement error;
* analysis choices;
* unrecognized confounding;
* local conditions;
* chance;
* fraud or mistakes.

A new investigation creates new [[Empirical evidence|empirical evidence]] and can show whether the original result depends strongly on one dataset, sample, laboratory, or context.

== What counts as a replication? ==
There is no perfectly sharp universal definition.

Replication studies can preserve or change:

* population;
* protocol;
* instrument;
* laboratory;
* experimenter;
* statistical model;
* location;
* time period.

The more conditions change, the more the study tests generality rather than exact repeatability.

The key question is whether the new study bears on substantially the same scientific claim.

== Direct and conceptual replication ==
Some literatures distinguish:

'''Direct replication''' — repeats the original procedure as closely as practical with new data.

'''Conceptual replication''' — tests the same theoretical claim using meaningfully different methods or operationalizations.

These labels are useful but field-dependent.

A close replication tests whether a particular operational result repeats.

A conceptual replication can provide stronger evidence that a theoretical relation is not an artifact of one procedure, but disagreement can arise over whether it truly tested the same claim.

== Failure to replicate ==
A replication that does not reproduce the original result is important evidence, but it does not automatically prove that the original claim is false.

Differences can arise from:
* sampling variation;
* low statistical power;
* changed population;
* procedural differences;
* measurement changes;
* context dependence;
* analysis choices;
* original false positive;
* replication false negative.

Interpretation requires comparing the studies rather than using "replicated" or "failed to replicate" as binary verdicts.

== Effect sizes ==
A scientifically useful replication asks not only whether a statistical significance threshold is crossed, but whether the estimated effect is consistent in direction and magnitude.

Confidence intervals, posterior distributions, and meta-analysis can compare estimates across studies.

A smaller but nonzero replicated effect can materially change the scientific conclusion even if the broad qualitative claim survives.

== Replication and generalization ==
Replication across diverse settings can reveal boundary conditions.

A result that appears only:
* in one population;
* at one laboratory;
* with one instrument;
* under one incentive structure;

may still be real but narrower than first claimed.

This turns replication into a tool for discovering scope, not merely policing mistakes.

== Replication and theory ==
A theory can survive an individual failed replication when other evidence remains strong or when a defensible boundary condition explains the discrepancy.

But repeated independent failures can weaken confidence substantially.

Theoretical adjustment should generate new testable consequences rather than merely shield the claim from every possible result.

== Multi-lab replication ==
Large collaborations can coordinate a common protocol across many laboratories.

Advantages include:
* larger samples;
* heterogeneous sites;
* direct estimates of between-site variation;
* reduced dependence on one lab.

However, coordinated replications can still share protocol assumptions and are not equivalent to many completely independent research traditions.

== Replication and meta-analysis ==
Meta-analysis combines evidence across studies.

It can estimate average effects and heterogeneity, but its conclusions depend on:
* study quality;
* publication bias;
* dependence among studies;
* model choice;
* comparability of measurements.

Replication creates the evidence base that meta-analysis can synthesize.

== Replication and reproducibility ==
Under the NASEM convention:

<pre>
Reproducibility → same data / same analysis
Replication     → new data / same scientific question
</pre>

A result can be computationally reproducible but fail replication.

A result can also replicate qualitatively even when the original computation cannot be exactly reproduced.

These diagnose different properties.

== Epistemic notes ==
Replication should update belief proportionally to study quality, independence, and comparability. One successful replication is not final proof, and one failed replication is not automatic refutation.

